<header><p class="st-kicker">county · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Clanricarde</h1><p class="st-meta">1333 CE – 1543 CE (approx.) · capital Loughrea</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Gaelicised Burke lordship of south Galway, ruled by the Mac William Uachtar, which became an earldom under Henry VIII in 1543.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-donn-de-burgh-3rd-earl-of-ulster">William Donn de Burgh</a>, Earl of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-ireland">Ulster</a>, was murdered in 1333, his <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-connacht">Connacht</a> lands were seized by his kinsmen. The southern branch, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulick-de-burgh">Ulick de Burgh</a>, adopted the Gaelic title Mac William Uachtar, the Upper Mac William, and ruled south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galway">Galway</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loughrea">Loughrea</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Clanricarde <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-burgh">Burkes</a> adopted Irish law, language and customs, intermarrying with Gaelic families while competing with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obrien-dynasty">O'Briens</a> and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galway">Galway</a> town. Their battles included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-knockdoe">Knockdoe</a> in 1504, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulick-fionn-burke">Ulick Fionn Burke</a> was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerald-fitzgerald-8th-earl-of-kildare">Earl of Kildare</a>. Tower houses dotted their lands.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1543 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulick-na-gceann-burke-1st-earl-of-clanricarde">Ulick na gCeann Burke</a> submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/earl-of-clanricarde">Earl of Clanricarde</a> in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland</a>. Later earls were loyal to the crown, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulick-burke-1st-marquess-of-clanricarde">Ulick Burke</a> was made Marquess of Clanricarde in 1646.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/clanricarde.jpg" alt="Clanricarde" width="320" height="357"><figcaption>Image: KINGHB190 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:De_burgh_arms.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1333 CE – 1543 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Loughrea</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ulick de Burgh, Ulick Fionn Burke, Ulick na gCeann Burke</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Irish, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ireland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mac William Uachtar, Upper Burkes</dd></dl></aside></div>
